              <td>Aggressive chemicals where silicone fails</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
        <h3>Is it normal for silicone tubing to wear out in weeks?</h3>
        <p>Only under heavy duty — high pressure, high speed, abrasive or incompatible media. Under gentle conditions the same tube should last hundreds of hours or more. If a tube fails quickly, check pressure, chemistry and speed before blaming the tube.</p>
        <h3>Can I extend tube life by using a harder tube?</h3>
        <p>Sometimes. Harder tubes (70–80 Shore A) resist pressure and abrasion better, but they must match the pump head's occlusion force — a head designed for soft tubes may not fully seal a hard one, which actually shortens effective life.</p>
        <h3>Should I replace tubing on a schedule or on signals?</h3>
        <p>Both. Use the signals (10% flow drop, cracks, discoloration) as the primary trigger, and use your logged hours to build a preventive schedule that changes tubes before the signals appear — especially in processes where downtime costs more than tubing.</p>
